a pair of B4s if needed.\n\nHmm. This is tricky. Let's consider the possible winning patterns again.\n\nIf I can form four chows and a pair, that's All Chows (24 points). To do that, I need:\n\n- W2-3-4 (need W4)\n- B2-3-4 (need B3)\n- T2-3-4 (need T3)\n- T7-8-9 (have T7 and T9, need T8, which is in the meld as a Pung)\nWait, the T8 is part of the Pung, so can't be used in a chow. So the T7-8-9 chow would need a T8 from the hand, but I don't have any. So that's not possible. So maybe only three chows.\n\nSo maybe three chows and a pair. That's not enough for All Chows. All Chows requires four chows and a pair. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form three chows and a pair, but that's not a valid pattern. So perhaps another pattern.\n\nWhat about Mixed Straight? Three chows in different suits. For example:\n\n- W2-3-4 (if I get W4)\n- B2-3-4 (if I get B3)\n- T2-3-4 (if I get T3)\n\nPlus a pair. That would be Mixed Straight (8 points). That's possible. If I can get those three chows and a pair, that's 8 points, which is the minimum to win.\n\nSo to achieve that, I need to keep the tiles that can form those chows. So I need to keep W2, W3, B2, B4, T2, T4. So if I discard a tile that's not part of these chows, like B9 or W9.\n\nSo if I discard B9, I can keep the other tiles for the chows. If I discard W9, same thing. So between B9 and W9, which is safer to discard?\n\nLooking at the unshown counts:\n\nB9 has 2 left, W9 has 3 left. So W9 is more abundant. Discarding W9 might be safer, as others are less likely to need it immediately. But if someone needs a W9 for a chow or pair, they might take it. But since I have a Pung of T8, which is a Bamboo tile, maybe others are focusing on different suits.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard B9, which is a terminal in Dots, someone might need it for their All Terminals or similar.\n\nAnother angle: the previous player has a meld of W6-7-8, which is a chow. The next player has a meld of W4-5-6. So the W suit is being used for 