 can form any melds. For example, if I get a W4, then W2-W3-W4. If I get a B3, then B2-B3-B4. If I get a T3, then T2-T3-T4. So three possible Chows. Plus the existing T8 Pung. Then, with those, I could have four melds (the Pung and three Chows) plus a pair. For example, if I get W4, B3, and T3, then:\n\n- Pung T8\n- Chow W2-W3-W4\n- Chow B2-B3-B4\n- Chow T2-T3-T4\n- Pair of T9 or B2 or B4.\n\nThat would be a winning hand with four melds and a pair. That's a standard hand. The points would depend on the patterns. For example, if all are Chows, that's All Chows (2 points). Plus other patterns like Half Flush if all are suited tiles and one honor. But since there are no honors in this case, maybe not. Also, if the tiles are from different suits, it's a Mixed Straight if they cover 1-9 in different suits. Wait, the example I thought of has three different suits (W, B, T). So that would be a Mixed Straight (8 points). Plus All Chows (2 points). Total 10 points, which is over 8, so valid.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T7, I can aim for that. So discarding T7 might be a good move.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T4, then I have T2 and T7 left. T2 needs T3 for a Chow, T7 needs T8 or T9. But T8 is already in a Pung, so T7-T8-T9 is possible if I have T9. But I have two T9s. So if I discard T4, maybe I can form a T7-T8-T9 Chow. But I have two T9s. So if I have T7 and T9, and another T8 (already in Pung), but the Pung is three T8s. So if I have T7 and T9, and a T8 from the Pung, but that's already part of the Pung. Not sure if that's allowed. Chows require three consecutive tiles in the same suit. So T7-T8-T9 is a valid Chow. But since I already have a Pung of T8, which is three T8s, maybe I can form a T7-T8-T9 Chow using one T8 from the Pung? Wait, no. The Pung is three T8s, which are part of the meld. To form a Chow, you need three tiles in sequence. If I have a T7 and T9, and a T8, but the T8 is already in the Pung, you can't use the same tile for both a Pung and a Chow. So 