 a terminal tile, which might be more likely to be needed by others for their hands. But if I discard T9, I have one left, which could be useful for a pair if I draw another. Alternatively, discarding B4 might not be as bad since there are four left.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, B4 has 4 left. So there's a higher chance of drawing another B4. If I discard B4, maybe I can get another one to form a pung. But I only have two, so even if I discard one, I can't form a pung unless I draw another two. That's unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T9, there are two left. If I draw one, I can have a pair of T9s. That might help. So maybe discarding T9 is better.\n\nBut how does that help the hand? If I have a pair of T9s, then I can have two pairs (B4x2 and T9x2). But need two more melds. For example, W2 and W3 could form a chow with W1 or W4. If I draw W4, then I can form a chow. But then the remaining tiles would be T4, J1. Need another meld. Not helpful.\n\nThis is really tough. Maybe the best I can do is discard a tile that allows me to wait on a useful tile. For example, if I discard T9, I can wait for another T9 or a W4. If I discard B4, I can wait for another B4. But since B4 has four left, maybe it's more likely to draw.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should discard J1, since it's a single and not part of any meld. That way, I have more suited tiles to work with.\n\nBut none of the options seem to lead to a winning hand. Perhaps I need to look for a different pattern. For example, maybe the hand has a concealed pung of B2 and T8, and the remaining tiles can form two more pungs. But no, I don't have enough.\n\nWait, maybe I can form a concealed kong of B4 if I draw another two. But that's four points, but I need to have four in hand. Not possible now.\n\nHmm. Maybe the best choice is to discard T9, as it's a duplicate and might be useful for a pair. So the answer would be Play T9.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has two pungs (T8 and B2) and potential for forming additional melds. Discarding T9 allows retaining one T9 for possible pairing and maintains flexibility in the hand.\nReason: Discarding T9 reduces redundancy and keeps the possibility of forming a T9 pair if another is drawn. The remaining tiles (W2, W3, T4, B4x2, J1) could form a pair of B4s and potentially two chows if W4 or T5/T6 are drawn, though the chances are low. This move maximizes future opportunities.\nAnswer: Play T9"}
